{"notice_id": "16552901", "title": "City Council Meeting | Boston.gov", "cancelled": false, "public_testimony": false, "notice_url": "https://www.boston.gov/public-notices/16552901", "posted_at": "2026-01-13T11:20:00-05:00", "event_datetime": "2026-08-05T16:00:00Z", "address_1": "Boston City Hall", "address_2": "Iannella Chamber", "page_text": "\n\n\n\n\n  APPROVAL OF THE JULY 22, 2026 CITY COUNCIL MEETING MINUTES\n\n\n\n\n\n  COMMUNICATIONS FROM HER HONOR, THE MAYOR:\n\n\n\n1513    Message and order authorizing the City of Boston to accept and expend the amount of One Hundred Thirty Thousand Dollars ($130,000.00) in the form of a grant, the HBS Leadership Fellow Grant, awarded by Harvard Business School to be administered by the Mayor's Office. The grant would fund the HBS Leadership Fellow position which will provide 1 year of support to the Mayor's Office.\n1514    Message and order to declare surplus City owned property currently under the management of the Mayor’s Office of Housing and transfer the care, custody, control and management thereof to the Public Facilities Commission. The Property comprises two (2) unnumbered parcels located at Melnea Cass Boulevard in the Roxbury District (Ward 09, Parcel #02225000, 02240000). The surplus vote will allow the City to later transfer the land to the Boston Planning and Development Agency (“BPDA”) pursuant to a 2002 Memorandum of Agreement between the City, BPDA, and MassDOT.\n1515    Message and order for the confirmation of the reappointment of Giovanny Valencia, as a Member of the Zoning Board of Appeal for a term expiring on May 1, 2027.\n1516    Message and order for the confirmation of the reappointment of Katie Whewell, as a Member of the Zoning Board of Appeal for a term expiring on May 1, 2027.\n1517    Message and order for the confirmation of the reappointment of Alan E. Langham, as a Member of the Zoning Board of Appeal for a term expiring on May 1, 2028.\n1518    Message and order for the confirmation of the reappointment of Sherry Dong, as a Member of the Zoning Board of Appeal for a term expiring on May 1, 2029.\n1519    Message and order for the confirmation of the reappointment of Shamaiah Turner, as a Member of the Zoning Board of Appeal for a term expiring on May 1, 2029.\n1520    Message and order for the confirmation of the reappointment of Jeanne Pinado, as an Alternate Member of the Zoning Board of Appeal for a term expiring on May 1, 2027.\n1521    Message and order for the confirmation of the reappointment of David Collins, as an Alternate Member of the Zoning Board of Appeal for a term expiring on May 1, 2028.\n1522    Message and order for the confirmation of the appointment of Carla Monteiro, as an Alternate Member of the Zoning Board of Appeal for a term expiring on May 1, 2028.\n1523    Message and order for the confirmation of the appointment of Patrick Walsh, as an Alternate Member of the Zoning Board of Appeal for a term expiring on November 1, 2028.\n1524    Message and order for the confirmation of the appointment of William Masterson, as an Alternate Member of the Zoning Board of Appeal for a term expiring on May 1, 2029.\n1525    Message and order for the confirmation of the appointment of renewal constables authorized to serve civil process upon the filing of their bonds for the period commencing May 1, 2026 and ending April 30, 2029.\n1526    Message and order for the confirmation of the appointment of a new Inspectional Services constable Carl C Horner authorized to serve civil process upon the filing of their bond for the period commencing May 1, 2025 and ending April 30, 2028.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  REPORTS OF PUBLIC OFFICERS AND OTHERS:\n\n\n\n1527    Notice was received from the Mayor of the appointment of Ava Callender Concepcion as a Member of the City of Boston Licensing Board, for a term expiring June 7, 2032.\n1528    Notice was received from the City Clerk in accordance with Chapter 6 of the Ordinances of 1979 regarding action taken by the Mayor on papers acted upon by the City Council at its meeting of July 8, 2026.\n1529    Notice was received from the City Clerk in accordance with Chapter 6 of the Ordinances of 1979 regarding action taken by the Mayor on papers acted upon by the City Council at its meeting of July 22, 2026.\n1530    Notice was received from the Auditing Department providing the Fiscal Year 2027 Payroll Allotment Schedules for all City Departments.\n1531    Notice was received from Robert Allison regarding his resignation as a Member of the Commemoration Commission, effective July 9, 2026.\n1532    Communication was received from Councilor Flynn regarding the selling of stolen goods and food, including meat, in the City of Boston.\n1533    Communication was received from Councilor Flynn regarding increases in phone theft, financial, and related scams.\n1534    Communication was received from Councilor Culpepper regarding the Medical Examiner's homicide determination in the death of Shacoby Kenny and the accompanying letter to the Acting Suffolk County Sheriff.\n1535    Communication was received from Councilor Murphy regarding a request for independent state oversight of Boston Public Schools transportation and Transdev.\n1536    Communication was received from Councilor Murphy regarding the requested briefing on Boston's Summer Public Safety Plan.\n1537    Communication was received from the Law Department regarding an Open Meeting Law complaint filed in City Council on July 8, 2026, Docket #1327.\n1538    Communication was received from Council President Liz Breadon to address an Open Meeting Law complaint and respond accordingly on behalf of the Boston City Council.\n1539    Communication was received by the City Clerk from the Boston Landmarks Commission for City Council action on the designation of The New England Conservatory, 290 Huntington Avenue, Boston, MA, as a Landmark (in effect after August 28, 2026 if not acted upon).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  MATTERS RECENTLY HEARD FOR POSSIBLE ACTION:\n\n\n\n1223    Message and order for your approval an Ordinance authorizing the City of Boston, acting through its Environment Department, to amend the Distributed Energy Resource Revenue Revolving Fund, 30396E. The current language for 30396E is limited in its use, and the changes proposed will allow for proper usage of 30396E while maintaining its original purpose. The amended language will allow the fund to properly capture revenues and expenditures, ensuring an enhanced and seamless financial process for current City energy activities. These amendments will properly capture demand response revenue and allow for the further development of the City's demand response program, increasing energy savings, and reduce the City's greenhouse gas emissions produced by emissions not addressed through the City's distributed energy resources.\n1316    Message and order for the confirmation of the appointment of Felicia Jaques as a member of the Boston Planning and Development Agency Board, for a term expiring August 15, 2029.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  MOTIONS, ORDERS AND RESOLUTIONS:\n\n\n\n1540    Councilor Worrell offered the following: Ordinance to Address Street Takeovers in the City of Boston.\n1541    Councilor Santana and Louijeune offered the following: Ordinance Establishing Minimum Training Requirements For Unarmed Security Guards.\n1542    Councilor Weber offered the following: Order for a hearing to review safety of the operation of heavy commercial vehicles and potential measures to protect pedestrians and cyclists in the City of Boston.\n1543    Councilor Durkan offered the following: Order for a hearing on traffic enforcement of blocked bike lanes and crosswalks.\n1544    Councilor Louijeune offered the following: Order for a hearing to discuss the future of Boston’s nighttime economy.\n1545    Councilor Worrell offered the following: Order for a hearing to explore a pilot program allowing the Boston Police Department to deploy noise cameras.\n1546    Councilor Flynn offered the following: Order for a hearing to examine services provided by the City of Boston to families of victims of violent crime and homicide.\n1547    Councilor Flynn offered the following: Order for a hearing to study a waiver from the residency requirement and allow for transfers from other municipalities to the Boston Police Department.\n1548    Councilor Flynn offered the following: Resolution in support of allowing retired police officers the opportunity to work paid details.\n1549    Councilor Flynn offered the following: Resolution in support of waiving the residency requirement to allow for transfers from other municipalities to the Boston Police Department.\n1550    Councilor Culpepper offered the following: Resolution calling for the Suffolk County Sheriff's Department to adopt critical incident administrative leave standards in light of the Medical Examiner's homicide determination in the death of Shacoby Kenny.\n1551    Councilor Culpepper offered the following: Resolution in support of S.2959, An Act Regarding Fair Housing Practices in the Commonwealth.\n1552    Councilor Louijeune offered the following: Resolution calling for an immediate housing stabilization response system for Boston residents who involuntarily lose work authorization due to federal changes in immigration policy.\n1553    Councilor Mejia offered the following: Resolution recognizing the importance of Teen Dating Violence awareness.\n1554    Councilor Murphy offered the following: Resolution supporting state and federal oversight of Transdev safety records and calling for greater transparency and accountability in Boston Public Schools transportation.\n1555    Councilor Breadon offered the following: Resolution recognizing National Farmers Market Week.\n1556    Councilor Murphy offered the following: Resolution recognizing National Health Center Week and honoring Boston's community health centers.\n1557    Councilor Worrell offered the following: Resolution recognizing August 6 as Jamaica Independence Day in the City of Boston.\n1558    Councilor Mejia offered the following: Resolution recognizing August 16, 2026 as Dominican Cultural Day.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  GREEN SHEETS:\n\n\n\nLegislative Calendar August 5, 2026.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  CONSENT AGENDA:\n\n\n\n1559    Councilor Durkan offered the following: Resolution recognizing 2026 Sox for Socks.\n1560    Councilor Durkan offered the following: Resolution recognizing James \"Jimmy\" Taylor.\n1561    Councilor Durkan offered the following: Resolution recognizing Commonwealth Shakespeare Company.\n1562    Councilor Durkan offered the following: Resolution recognizing Natalia Torbina.\n1563    Councilor Durkan and Santana offered the following: Resolution declaring July 25, 2026 as \"Mission Main Family Fun Day\" in the City of Boston.\n1564    Councilor Murphy offered the following: Resolution recognizing Susan L. Curry.\n1565    Councilor FitzGerald offered the following: Resolution in memory of William John Yamarick.\n1566    Councilor FitzGerald offered the following: Resolution in memory of Marek Eugeniusz Konka.\n1567    Councilor FitzGerald offered the following: Resolution recognizing Cecilia Abraham.\n1568    Councilor FitzGerald offered the following: Resolution recognizing Caribbean Carnival Contributors.\n1569    Councilor FitzGerald, Durkan and Santana offered the following: Resolution recognizing John Jackson.\n1570    Councilor Flynn offered the following: Resolution recognizing Mary Ellen McCormack Community.\n1571    Councilor Flynn offered the following: Resolution recognizing Gallivan Boulevard Residents and Community.\n1572    Councilor Culpepper offered the following: Resolution recognizing Gordon Thompson.\n1573    Councilor Culpepper offered the following: Resolution recognizing Moses Borders.\n1574    Councilor Culpepper offered the following: Resolution recognizing Kool DJ Red Alert.\n1575    Councilor Culpepper offered the following: Resolution recognizing Leland Clar